---
/**
 * The site header - mark on the left, action on the right.
 *
 * `design-system.md` §9 has always listed a nav in the component inventory; `phases.md`
 * never scheduled one, so the site ran to the end of 8.2 with no header at all and the
 * mark nowhere on the page. That was a gap in the plan rather than a decision.
 *
 * **It is out of flow rather than in it**, which is the one thing here worth explaining.
 * The hero's fold is `100svh` and its sticky pin is aligned to the viewport; putting a
 * header in normal flow above it would push the fold past the bottom of the screen and
 * shift every device offset the section was gated on. Overlaying it costs the hero
 * nothing and keeps 8.2's approved geometry exactly as it was measured.
 *
 * **It is fixed** (owner, Gate C rev 13), so the mark and the action stay reachable for
 * the whole scroll rather than leaving with the first screen. It is anchored to the
 * viewport's corners - no content column, no auto margins - so the mark sits in the
 * top-left corner itself and the action in the top-right, each held off the edge by
 * padding alone.
 *
 * **There is deliberately no navigation, and this is now a decision rather than a wait.**
 * The comment here used to say links would appear once pricing and the content routes gave
 * them somewhere to go. They did, and 8.10e built it: three links - Pricing, Blog,
 * Changelog - with `aria-current` marking, a zero-JavaScript `<details>` disclosure below
 * 768, and the whole three-gate cycle behind it. **The owner rejected it at Gate C**
 * (2026-09-10): *"I don't like the links being in the header. Remove them completely, so
 * they are only on the footer."*
 *
 * So the header is the mark and one action, permanently. `design-system.md` §9's inventory
 * no longer lists a nav, and `phases.md` 8.10e records what was built and removed. **Do
 * not add one back as an oversight** - it is the one thing on this site that has been
 * built, measured against four references, and turned down.
 *
 * **The action is dropped on the page it points at** (owner, 8.8's Gate C): "Join the
 * waitlist" sitting in the corner of `/waitlist` is an invitation to somewhere you already
 * are, and a second control competing with the form for the same click. On that route the
 * header is the mark alone.
 *
 * It is derived from the path rather than passed in as a prop. Every page renders this
 * through `Base.astro`, so a prop would have to be threaded through the layout by each
 * route and could be forgotten by the next one - where "don't advertise the page you are
 * on" is a rule the component can simply know. It applies to the action and to nothing
 * else, there being nothing else here.
 */
import Wordmark from './brand/Wordmark.astro';
import Button from './primitives/Button.astro';

const WAITLIST = '/waitlist';

// Trailing slashes vary with Astro's build config; the comparison should not.
const here = Astro.url.pathname.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '/';
const showsWaitlistAction = here !== WAITLIST;

/**
 * The scroll scrim is on every route **except the home page at `md` and up** (owner, 8.9e's
 * Gate C). Below `md` the home page has it too (owner, 8.14c round 5: the logo and the
 * action overlap the acts' content on a phone) - see the `.home` rule in the styles.
 *
 * It was added at 8.9c for a collision the blog has: a post's cover is a 700px image
 * carrying its own white display type, so a hook and the wordmark land at the same size,
 * in the same colour, on the same pixels. **`/` does not have that problem**, and it is
 * the one route where the scrim itself is the thing you notice - it is the site's only
 * full-bleed composition, so a band darkening as you scroll reads *as a band* across the
 * hero and the four acts, where over a column of prose it reads as nothing at all.
 *
 * Derived from the path, like `showsWaitlistAction` above and for the same reason: every
 * page renders this through `Base.astro`, so a prop would have to be threaded through the
 * layout by each route and could be forgotten by the next one.
 */
const onHome = here === '/';

import { catalogue } from '../i18n';

const t = catalogue(Astro.currentLocale);
---

<header class:list={['site-header', 'scrimmed', { home: onHome }]}>
  <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label={t.ui.homeLabel}>
    {
      /*
      30 here, not 35: `Wordmark` sizes the mark at 1.15x the word so the lockup's
      proportion is one number rather than two that can drift, and 30 x 1.15 rounds to the
      35px mark asked for. Setting the mark alone would have broken that tie.
    */
    }
    <Wordmark variant="lockup" size={30} />
  </a>

  {
    /*
    The header owns the conversion now. Because it is fixed, "Join the waitlist" is on
    screen for the whole page, which is what freed the hero to carry "See how it works"
    instead - the two calls to action no longer compete and the duplicate is gone.

    It points at `/waitlist`, a route, since 8.8: the owner chose a page over a closing
    band on the home page at Gate A. So this is a navigation, not the in-page scroll the
    `#waitlist` anchor used to be - and it is the only thing on the site that makes that
    route reachable, which is why the header is not left without navigation twice.

    On `/waitlist` itself it is absent. See the frontmatter.
  */
  }
  {
    showsWaitlistAction && (
      <Button href={WAITLIST} variant="ghost">
        {t.ui.header.action}
      </Button>
    )
  }
</header>

<style>
  .site-header {
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    /* Above the hero's bloom and its sticky device, both of which own lower layers. */
    z-index: 10;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--spacing-4);
    /* No content column and no auto margins: the mark belongs in the corner of the
     * viewport, not in the corner of the text column, and padding is the only thing
     * holding either end off the edge. */
    padding: var(--spacing-6);
  }

  /*
   * The scrim - added at 8.9c's Gate C rev 2 (owner: *"the text clashes with the Sonaru
   * logo"*).
   *
   * **The header has been transparent since 8.2 and every page's content has always passed
   * under it.** It only became visible on the blog, because that is the first route whose
   * content is a 700px image carrying its own white display type - the wordmark and a
   * post's hook line up at the same size, in the same colour, on the same pixels. The owner
   * asked whether the page needed more padding; it does not. At rest the masthead already
   * clears the 92px header by 36px. The collision happens *while scrolling*, which no
   * amount of padding reaches.
   *
   * So the header stops being transparent once there is something behind it. It is a
   * gradient rather than a bar because a hard-edged strip across the top of a page is a
   * second horizontal seam, and this site has already spent two gate rounds removing one.
   *
   * **It is off on `/` - see `showsScrim` in the frontmatter.** The home route is the one
   * page this was never for: its content is a composition rather than a column, and the
   * band is legible there as a band. 8.9c reasoned about the scrim only from the blog,
   * which is where it was needed and where it is invisible.
   *
   * **No JavaScript**: `animation-timeline: scroll()` drives the opacity from the document's
   * own scroll position, so the blog routes keep the zero-byte budget
   * `tests/e2e/blog.spec.ts` asserts. Where that is unsupported the `@supports` block below
   * leaves the scrim on permanently - the fallback is the safe state, not the broken one.
   */
  .site-header.scrimmed::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    /* Past the header's own box, so the gradient finishes feathering below the lockup
     * rather than being cut off at it. */
    inset: 0 0 calc(-1 * var(--spacing-8)) 0;
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ground-solid) 88%, transparent) 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ground-solid) 58%, transparent) 52%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    opacity: 0;
    animation: header-scrim linear both;
  }

  /*
   * **The timeline lives in its own rule, and that is a fix, not tidiness** (8.14). Written as
   * a longhand beside the shorthand above, the build's minifier folded it in and shipped
   * `animation: linear both header-scrim scroll()` - which iOS Safari 26 rejects as a whole,
   * so on every iPhone the scrim had no animation at all and sat at `opacity: 0`. A separate
   * rule cannot be merged into the one above. `tests/unit/built-css.spec.ts` checks the
   * shipped bytes, because no browser the suite runs reproduces the rejection.
   */
  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    .site-header.scrimmed::before {
      animation-timeline: scroll();
      /*
       * Fully in by the time the page has travelled the header's own height - the distance
       * after which content is genuinely underneath it rather than approaching it.
       */
      animation-range: 0 92px;
    }
  }

  @keyframes header-scrim {
    to {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  @supports not (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    .site-header.scrimmed::before {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  /* The home page's exception, at `md` and up only (8.9e; narrowed at 8.14c round 5). After
   * the scrim rule, which it must out-order at equal specificity. */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .site-header.home::before {
      content: none;
    }
  }

  /*
   * **No reduced-motion override, and the reason is worth stating** because
   * `design-system.md` §5 would otherwise apply: a scrubbed signal is replaced by its final
   * state, never removed.
   *
   * That rule exists so a visitor who cannot take motion is never left with content the
   * scrub was hiding. Nothing here is hidden either way - the scrim's whole job is to sit
   * *behind* the lockup. And it does not move: no transform, no translation, no parallax,
   * just a background opacity tracking scroll position, which is the class of thing reduced
   * motion is not about.
   *
   * Pinning it on was tried first at 8.9c and is measurably worse: it darkens the top 120px
   * of every route at rest, where on most of them there is nothing behind it to darken.
   * Measured then as 38,000-168,000 changed pixels across fifteen frozen reduced-motion
   * baselines, every one confined to rows 0-122.
   *
   * **8.9e took the argument further than the override.** Most of that measurement was the
   * home route's five sections, and they no longer carry the scrim in either motion mode -
   * the owner removed it there outright. What the reasoning still covers is `/pricing` and
   * `/waitlist`, which keep the scrim and have nothing behind the header either; the
   * override stays absent for them on the same grounds.
   */

  .brand {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* The mark is the link's whole content; no underline, and no colour change on hover -
     * a logo that recolours reads as broken rather than as interactive. */
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: var(--spacing-2);
  }

  /* The lockup is already sized by its own prop; this only softens the hover. */
  .brand:hover {
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .site-header {
      padding: var(--spacing-4);
    }
  }
</style>
